## What is changing

Recent, repository-backed remote Copilot SDK sessions now appear in the Agents window alongside eligible local Copilot CLI and GitHub Copilot app sessions.

Before, Agent Host could enumerate remote sessions but rejected every one as having an unsupported or missing client name. Opening a surfaced remote session now connects it lazily through the SDK and preserves the resolved working directory so its transcript can resume immediately and after Agent Host restarts.

## Why

The exported logs showed the startup catalog listing five SDK sessions and classifying all five as unsupported or missing-client sessions. By contrast, `availability inquiry` appeared through a later live `root/sessionAdded` notification after Agent Host already owned that session, so it bypassed external discovery.

The SDK models local and remote catalog entries as separate variants. Local entries may contain `clientName`; remote entries intentionally do not and instead carry task and repository metadata. The existing classifier unconditionally converted every remote entry's client name to `undefined`, making the remote variant impossible to discover.

## How

- request both local and remote SDK catalog entries;
- retain the local `github/cli` and `github/autopilot` client allowlist while validating remote entries through their remote repository metadata;
- cache remote metadata before known-session filtering so persisted rows can restore reliably;
- call `sessions.connect` only when the user opens a remote session, then persist its working directory and connected SDK backing ID;
- cover discovery, metadata projection, lazy connection, and immediate transcript resume in the focused Copilot Agent tests.

Fixes #332185
